{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2003 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ba68cd52eec8083254e6cde06857e91a96d53f3f18c2b8b616b4fbe345117f2a" :edb/db-before-t 2002 :edb/db-after-t 2003 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535315 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:53:31.204Z" 13194139535315 true] [17592186149732 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149733 13194139535315 true] [17592186149736 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149738 13194139535315 true] [17592186149740 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149741 13194139535315 true] [17592186149754 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149756 13194139535315 true] [17592186149757 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149758 13194139535315 true] [17592186149760 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149761 13194139535315 true] [17592186151830 1100 "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2$." 13194139535315 false] [17592186151830 1100 "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186151830 1145 "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/assets/8c9f75d71e362ec7207e00f67adecae7193e35972e46a783faa5aa1ea6c00301.png)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186151830 1145 "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= 2x(x^{2})^{′} - x^{2}(2x)^{′} \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} - 2x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535315 false] [17592186151831 1100 "$$\ny_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad\ny_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139535315 true] [17592186151831 1100 "$y_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad y_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R$\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139535315 false] [17592186151831 1145 "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I$.\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I$, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + 1 & 2x \\\\ (x^{2} + 1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + 1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 1) \\cdot 2 - (2x) \\cdot (2x) \\\\\n&= 2(x^{2} + 1) - 4x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + 2 - 4x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore, the set\n\n$$\n\\left\\{y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nis linearly independent on $\\mathbb R$." 13194139535315 false] [17592186151831 1145 "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/assets/19eae90f8f511056865f967ce8bf784261bd0f1b20cec65f478919e4b1c00078.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186151832 1100 "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R$.\n2. $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0$.\n3. The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$." 13194139535315 false] [17592186151832 1100 "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\n- $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186151832 1145 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n$y_{1}^{′}(x)|= 3x^{2}; y_{1}^{″}(x)|= 6x$\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n$x^{2} \\cdot 6x - 5x \\cdot 3x^{2} + 9x^{3}|= 0; 6x^{3} - 15x^{3} + 9x^{3}|= 0; 0|= 0✓$\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n$y_{2}^{′}(x)|= 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}; y_{2}^{″}(x)|= 6x\\ln x + 5x$\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n$x^{2}(6x\\ln x + 5x) - 5x(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) + 9x^{3}\\ln x|= 0; (6x^{3}\\ln x + 5x^{3} - 15x^{3}\\ln x - 5x^{3} + 9x^{3}\\ln x)|= 0; 0|= 0✓$\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n$W(y_{1}, y_{2})|=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3} & x^{3}\\ln x \\\\ 3x^{2} & 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}\\end{vmatrix}; = x^{3}(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) - x^{3}\\ln x \\cdot 3x^{2}; = 3x^{5}\\ln x + x^{5} - 3x^{5}\\ln x; = x^{5}$\nSince the Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty)$, the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535315 false] [17592186151832 1145 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535315 true] [17592186151833 1100 "Given the set of functions $\\{ e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ explain why they are linearly independent." 13194139535315 true] [17592186151833 1100 "Given the set of functions $\\{e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, explain why they are linearly independent." 13194139535315 false] [17592186151833 1145 "Recall that the set of functions $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ Otherwise, if $k_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)=0 \\\\  & {k}_{1}\\cdot 1+{k}_{2}\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{1})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)=0 \\\\  & e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\,⇒\\,e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}.$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186151833 1145 "Recall that the set of functions $\\{f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$, which are defined on some interval $I$, is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e,\n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I$. Otherwise, if\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0\n$$\n\nis the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have that\n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get\n$k_{1}(e^{0}) + k_{2}(\\sin 0) = 0; k_{1} \\cdot 1 + k_{2} \\cdot 0 = 0⇒k_{1} = 0$.\n- If $x=1$, we get\n$k_{1}(e^{1}) + k_{2}(\\sin 1) = 0; ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0⇒ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0$.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{k_{1} = 0; ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}$." 13194139535315 false] [17592186152202 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343308 13194139535315 true] [17592186152203 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343288 13194139535315 true] [17592186152203 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343295 13194139535315 true] [17592186152203 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343396 13194139535315 true] [17592186152203 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343403 13194139535315 true] [17592186152203 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343410 13194139535315 true] [17592186152204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343327 13194139535315 true] [17592186152204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343339 13194139535315 true] [17592186152204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343351 13194139535315 true] [17592186152204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343363 13194139535315 true] [17592186152205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343375 13194139535315 true] [17592186152205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343382 13194139535315 true] [17592186152205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343389 13194139535315 true] [17592186152482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343302 13194139535315 true] [17592186152482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343305 13194139535315 true] [17592186152482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535315 true] [17592186152482 1100 "Given the set of functions ${x, e^{x}, 2x}$ defined on $x ∈ R$, select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x ∈ R$, we have that\n$2$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139535315 false] [17592186152482 1100 "Given the set of functions ${x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,2x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." 13194139535315 true] [17592186152482 1145 "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186152484 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535315 true] [17592186152484 1100 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186152484 1100 "$y_{1}(x) = e^{2x + 1}, y_{2}(x) = e^{2x}, x ∈ R$\nConsider the functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ above. Given that $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(y_{1}, y_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$." 13194139535315 false] [17592186152484 1145 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186152486 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535315 true] [17592186152486 1100 "$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139535315 true] [17592186152486 1100 "$y^{″} + y = 0$\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = \\sin (x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_{2}(x) = 3\\sin (x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139535315 false] [17592186152486 1145 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535315 true] [17592186152487 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535315 true] [17592186152487 1100 "$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139535315 true] [17592186152487 1100 "$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 6y = 0$\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = e^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_{2}(x) = 1 + e^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139535315 false] [17592186152487 1145 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6(1+{e}^{-x}) & =0 \\\\ 6 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535315 true] [17592186343288 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343289 13194139535315 true] [17592186343288 1089 "q-129797" 13194139535315 true] [17592186343288 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535315 true] [17592186343288 1100 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186343288 1113 #uuid "a13de3fe-2f2e-5ac1-a84a-1f6d262b4edc" 13194139535315 true] [17592186343288 1145 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){(x)}^{′} \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535315 true] [17592186343289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343290 13194139535315 true] [17592186343289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343291 13194139535315 true] [17592186343289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343292 13194139535315 true] [17592186343289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343293 13194139535315 true] [17592186343289 1000 #edb/re