in{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343384 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343385 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343386 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343387 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343388 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1041 "selection" 13194139535315 true] [17592186343383 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343388 13194139535315 true] [1759218